<p>The estimate is the foundation of every property claim. It decides how many squares of roofing get paid, whether a room gets new drywall or just paint, and how much is held back as depreciation. Yet most policyholders receive a 20- or 40-page document full of abbreviations and line codes with little explanation. An <strong>insurance estimate review in Port Arthur</strong> translates that document into plain English, compares it to the actual damage and identifies items that are missing, understated or priced incorrectly. We review estimates for homeowners, landlords and businesses across Port Arthur, Nederland, Groves, Port Neches and the rest of Jefferson County.</p>

<h2>How to Read an Insurance Estimate</h2>
<p>Most carrier estimates are written in Xactimate and follow the same structure:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Areas or rooms.</strong> Roof, elevations (exterior walls), each interior room, other structures and sometimes contents.</li>
<li><strong>Line items.</strong> Each task, such as removing and replacing shingles or painting a wall, with a quantity, unit and unit price.</li>
<li><strong>Depreciation.</strong> A deduction on many line items for the age and condition of the material.</li>
<li><strong>Summary page.</strong> Replacement cost value, depreciation, deductible, prior payments and the net amount paid.</li>
</ul>
<p>Xactimate unit prices are regional and updated periodically, so the price list date on the estimate matters, especially after a major storm when Southeast Texas labor and material costs can rise quickly.</p>

