<h2>Code Items and Port Arthur Requirements</h2>
<p>The City of Port Arthur enforces the 2021 International Residential Code and 2021 International Building Code, and re-roofs and structural repairs generally require a permit. In Jefferson County, roof work must also meet TDI windstorm installation requirements and be certified with a WPI-8 to keep TWIA eligibility. Those requirements can add materials, labor and inspection steps that a basic estimate omits. Code-driven costs are paid only if the policy includes ordinance or law coverage, and only up to that limit, so the review checks your declarations as well as the estimate. Our <a href="/local-building-codes/">local building codes</a> page has city contact details.</p>

<h2>Depreciation: Recoverable or Not?</h2>
<p>Depreciation is often the largest single deduction on an estimate. The review looks at three things:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Is the amount reasonable?</strong> Depreciation should reflect the actual age and condition of the material, not a blanket percentage.</li>
<li><strong>Is it recoverable?</strong> Under replacement cost coverage, depreciation is typically released after repairs are completed, often within a deadline in the policy.</li>
<li><strong>Does a roof schedule apply?</strong> Some Texas policies pay roofs at actual cash value only or use a payment schedule based on roof age, which changes what is owed.</li>
</ol>
<p>The deductible deserves a check too. Named-storm and wind/hail deductibles are often a percentage of Coverage A, and homes with separate TWIA and homeowners policies may have two. Our <a href="/hurricane-deductible-calculator/">hurricane deductible calculator</a> and the article on <a href="/hurricane-deductible-texas-explained/">Texas hurricane deductibles</a> explain the math.</p>

<h2>Comparing a Contractor Bid With the Carrier Estimate</h2>
<p>Homeowners often hold a contractor bid in one hand and the insurer’s estimate in the other, with a large gap between them and no way to explain it. The two documents are usually built differently: a bid may be a single lump sum, while the carrier estimate is itemized by line. Converting the bid’s scope into the same line-item format shows whether the difference comes from missing work, different quantities, unit pricing or overhead and profit. Sometimes the bid includes upgrades the policy will not pay for; sometimes the estimate leaves out work the contractor cannot avoid. Either way, a like-for-like comparison is the only way to know.</p>

<h2>What a Review Produces</h2>
<p>After a review, you should understand what the carrier’s estimate covers, where it differs from the damage on site and roughly how significant those differences are. Where the gaps are real, we prepare a supporting Xactimate estimate with photos and measurements so the insurer can compare its numbers directly. That documentation is the basis for a <a href="/services/insurance-claim-supplements/">claim supplement</a>, a response to an <a href="/services/underpaid-insurance-claims/">underpaid claim</a> or, if the disagreement persists, <a href="/services/insurance-appraisal/">insurance appraisal</a>. Sometimes the review confirms the estimate is fair, which is also worth knowing before you sign a repair contract.</p>

<h2>Why Have a Port Arthur Public Adjuster Review the Estimate</h2>
<p>A Port Arthur Public Adjuster reviews estimates on behalf of the policyholder, with no stake in keeping the number low. We combine the estimate review with an on-site inspection and, where helpful, moisture meters, thermal imaging and Matterport 3D imaging to document what is really there. We cannot guarantee that a review will change the payment, but it gives you a clear picture of the claim. Fees are disclosed in a written contract before any work begins; Texas law caps public adjuster compensation at 10% of the insurance settlement. Send your estimate through a <a href="/free-claim-review/">Free Claim Review</a> or call [[phone]].</p>
